irect `_cmp` calls are the most reliable. But maybe mixing in operator-based tests adds robustness. I'll primarily use direct `_cmp` calls, and maybe add a couple of operator-level assertions as postcondition checks.\n\nHmm, but careful: if `__lt__` etc. are defined in terms of `_cmp`, then `v1 < v2` calls `_cmp`. That's fine.\n\nLet me now write the final test script. But first, the get_info calls.\n\nLet me think about what could differ. The ansible module_utils/compat/version.py \u2014 I recall it's a vendored copy of distutils version.py with `StringTypes` replaced. Let me check `Version` class methods via get_info.\n\nOK, making the calls now.\n\nAfter getting info, I'll write:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for ansible.module_utils.compat.version.StrictVersion._cmp.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.compat.version import StrictVersion\n\n\nclass _TruthFlipFlop:\n    \"\"\"Truthiness helper whose boolean value flips on every evaluation.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, values):\n        self._values = list(values)\n        self._calls = 0\n\n    def __bool__(self):\n        value = self._values[self._calls % len(self._values)]\n        self._calls += 1\n        return value\n\n\ndef test_cmp_string_operand():\n    \"\"\"_cmp parses a string operand (lines 170-171).\"\"\"\n    version = StrictVersion('1.2')\n    assert version._cmp('1.2') == 0\n    assert version._cmp('1.3') == -1\n\n\ndef test_cmp_unsupported_type_returns_not_implemented():\n    \"\"\"_cmp returns NotImplemented for non-StrictVersion operands (lines 172-173).\"\"\"\n    version = StrictVersion('1.2')\n    assert version._cmp(object()) is NotImplemented\n    assert version._cmp(None) is NotImplemented\n\n\ndef test_cmp_older_version_returns_negative():\n    assert StrictVersion('1.2')._cmp(StrictVersion('2.0')) == -1\n\n\ndef test_cmp_newer_version_returns_positive():\n    assert StrictVersion('2.0')._cmp(StrictVersion('1.2')) == 1\n\n\ndef test_cmp_equal_versions_without_prerelease(